{"test_cases_lookup_map": {"{\"actual_output\": \"(generator not evaluated in this run)\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": \"A refspec tells Git which remote reference corresponds to which local reference when you fetch or push.\\n\\n* **Fetch** \\u2013 The refspec\\u202f`+refs/heads/<remote\\u2011branch>:refs/remotes/<remote>/<remote\\u2011branch>` copies the remote branch\\u202f`<remote\\u2011branch>` into a local remote\\u2011tracking branch `refs/remotes/<remote>/<remote\\u2011branch>`. The \\u201c+\\u201d forces an update even if it\\u2019s a non\\u2011fast\\u2011forward.\\n\\n* **Push** \\u2013 The refspec\\u202f`refs/heads/<local\\u2011branch>:refs/heads/<remote\\u2011branch>` sends your local branch\\u202f`<local\\u2011branch>` to the remote, creating or updating the remote branch\\u202f`<remote\\u2011branch>`.\\n\\nThus, when you add a new remote branch, the fetch refspec maps the remote\\u2019s branch to a local remote\\u2011tracking branch, and the push refspec maps your local branch to the remote branch you want to update.\", \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"Imagine adding a remote branch; how would a refspec map references during fetch and push?\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"Git Refspec\\n\\nA refspec describes how references are mapped during operations such as fetch and push. It specifies relationships between local and remote references.\\n\\nFor example, remote-tracking branch updates are based on configured fetch refspecs. Understanding refspecs becomes useful when working with advanced Git workflows, multiple remotes, selective fetching, or unusual branch mappings.\\n\\nGit Aliases\\n\\nGit aliases provide custom shortcuts for Git commands. An alias can simplify frequently repeated commands or create convenient combinations of options.\\n\\nFor example, developers commonly create a short alias for a graph-style git log. Aliases are personal configuration and do not change the underlying Git behavior.\\n\\nGit Clean\\n\\ngit clean rem