_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Recall", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "openai/gpt-oss-20b", "evaluationCost": 0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "openai/gpt-oss-20b", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}, {"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Precision", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "openai/gpt-oss-20b", "evaluationCost": 0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7, "evaluation_model": "openai/gpt-oss-20b", "strict_mode": false, "include_reason": true}}], "cached_classifications": []}, "{\"actual_output\": \"(generator not evaluated in this run)\", \"context\": null, \"expected_output\": \"Git\\u2019s object database (the repository) stores the complete commit history locally in every clone. Because each clone contains its own full object DB, you can run\\u202f`git bisect` entirely offline\\u2014no remote access is needed. The workflow is simply:\\n\\n1. Ensure the clone has the relevant commits (the full history is already present).  \\n2. Use `git bisect start`, `git bisect good`, and `git bisect bad` to narrow down the offending commit.  \\n\\nSince the repository database holds all objects, the bisect operates independently of any remote, even across separate clones.\", \"hyperparameters\": null, \"input\": \"Investigate Git's object DB enabling offline bisect across independent clones.\", \"retrieval_context\": [\"Developers working with very large repositories may use shallow clones, sparse checkout, partial clone, or other advanced features to reduce local data requirements.\\n\\nShallow Clone\\n\\nA shallow clone downloads only a limited portion of repository history. For example, a clone can be configured to retrieve only recent commits.\\n\\nShallow clones can reduce download size and speed up CI operations, but they may not contain enough history for operations requiring older commits.\\n\\nSparse Checkout\\n\\nSparse checkout allows only selected portions of a repository's working tree to be populated. It can be useful in large monorepositories where a developer needs only a subset of files.\\n\\nSparse checkout affects the working tree rather than changing the repository's complete history.\\n\\nGit Conflict Resolution Workflow\", \"Developers working with very large repositories may use shallow clones, sparse checkout, partial clone, or other advanced features to reduce local data requirements.\\n\\nShallow Clone\\n\\nA shallow clone downloads only a limited portion of repository history. For example, a clone can be configured to retrieve only recent commits.\\n\\nShallow clones can reduce download size and speed up CI operations, but they may not contain enough history for operations requiring older commits.\\n\\nSparse Checkout\\n\\nSparse checkout allows only selected portions of a repository's working tree to be populated. It can be useful in large monorepositories where a developer needs only a subset of files.\\n\\nSparse checkout affects the working tree rather than changing the repository's complete history.\\n\\nGit Conflict Resolution Workflow\", \"Developers working with very large repositories may use shallow clones, sparse checkout, partial clone, or other advanced features to reduce local data requirements.\\n\\nShallow Clone\\n\\nA shallow clone downloads only a limited portion of repository history. For example, a clone can be configured to retrieve only recent commits.\\n\\nShallow clones can reduce download size and speed up CI operations, but they may not contain enough history for operations requiring older commits.\\n\\nSparse Checkout\\n\\nSparse checkout allows only selected portions of a repository's working tree to be populated. It can be useful in large monorepositories where a developer needs only a subset of files.\\n\\nSparse checkout affects the working tree rather than changing the repository's 