 most common remote name is origin. git remote -v displays configured remote URLs. git remote add adds a remote. git remote remove removes one. git remote rename changes its name.\\n\\nRemote configuration includes information such as the remote URL and fetch configuration. Git documentation describes remote.origin.url and remote.origin.fetch as important parts of remote configuration.\\n\\nGit Fetch\\n\\ngit fetch downloads changes and references from a remote repository without integrating those changes into the current working branch. It updates remote-tracking references such as origin/main.\\n\\nFetching is useful because it allows developers to inspect remote changes before deciding whether to merge, rebase, or otherwise integrate them.\", \"Tags are useful for identifying stable release versions because a tag provides a human-readable reference to a specific commit.\\n\\nGit Remote\\n\\nA remote represents another repository that a local repository communicates with. The most common remote name is origin. git remote -v displays configured remote URLs. git remote add adds a remote. git remote remove removes one. git remote rename changes its name.\\n\\nRemote configuration includes information such as the remote URL and fetch configuration. Git documentation describes remote.origin.url and remote.origin.fetch as important parts of remote configuration.\\n\\nGit Fetch\\n\\ngit fetch downloads changes and references from a remote repository without integrating those changes into the current working branch. It updates remote-tracking references such as origin/main.\\n\\nFetching is useful because it allows developers to inspect remote changes before deciding whether to merge, rebase, or otherwise integrate them.\"]}": {"cached_metrics_data": [{"metric_data": {"name": "Contextual Recall", "threshold": 0.7, "success": false, "strictMode": false, "flaky": false, "evaluationModel": "openai/gpt-oss-20b", "evaluationCost": 0}, "metric_configuration": {"threshold": 0.7,